{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1779201669,"nonce":"FH3bU7ycmh+iH6zVX4d186NAYx4=","message":"Selector/runtime-schema dependency noted from .\n\nThe current Studio selector work can only provide two partial layers:\n- curated semantic shortcuts for known structures, e.g. effector position/velocity;\n- a generic stored subpath/slice expression relative to a selected source, e.g. .\n\nThat is enough to represent user intent in a durable objective spec, but it is not the full UX implied by a real state browser. A proper selector browser depends on provider/schema introspection from this contract: component output schemas, PyTree leaf paths, array shapes/dtypes/axis labels, named fields/keys, and validation/lowering hooks for selector expressions.\n\nWhen this provider/schema contract grows runtime schema introspection, revisit  and update Studio so objective/probe targeting can browse actual PyTree leaves and array axes, validate field/index/slice expressions before use, and lower selectors deterministically into runtime getters. This should replace the current curated-hints-first behavior for known examples and make arbitrary component outputs targetable without hardcoding every port structure.","files":null}]}